
## Septic Cardiomyopathy (Sepsis-Induced Cardiomyopathy, SICM)

**Septic cardiomyopathy** (also termed **sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy** or **sepsis-induced myocardial dysfunction**) is a **reversible, acute cardiac dysfunction** occurring in patients with sepsis, characterized by **systolic and/or diastolic impairment of one or both ventricles**, **unrelated to coronary artery disease or pre-existing cardiac pathology**. [^1_1][^1_2][^1_3][^1_4] It is a **common complication of severe sepsis and septic shock**, with reported incidence ranging from **20–60%** in ICU patients, and is associated with **significantly increased mortality** (up to 70–90% in some series). [^1_5][^1_6][^1_7]

***

## Definition \& Diagnostic Criteria

There is **no universally accepted consensus definition**, but key features are widely agreed upon: [^1_1][^1_8][^1_3][^1_4]

### Core Definition

- **Acute, reversible myocardial dysfunction** (systolic and/or diastolic)
- **Unrelated to ischemia** (non-coronary etiology)
- **Occurs in the setting of sepsis/septic shock**
- **Not attributable to pre-existing cardiac disease**


### Commonly Used Echocardiographic Criteria

| Parameter | Typical Threshold |
| :-- | :-- |
| **LVEF** | <40–50% (some use <45%) [^1_2][^1_8][^1_6] |
| **LV dilation** | Increased end-diastolic volume (EDV) with **normal or low filling pressures** [^1_5][^1_2][^1_9] |
| **RV dysfunction** | RV dilation, reduced FAC (fractional area change) [^1_8][^1_6] |
| **Diastolic dysfunction** | Impaired relaxation/filling on Doppler [^1_10][^1_9] |
| **Reversibility** | Recovery within **7–10 days** in survivors [^1_5][^1_2][^1_9] |

### Clinical Features Suggestive of SICM [^1_2]

- Hemodynamic instability **despite vasopressor therapy**
- Failure to respond to **preload challenge** (fluid unresponsiveness)
- **"Cold" extremities** in septic patient
- **Elevated cardiac troponins** (without ACS)
- **Arrhythmias** (new-onset AF, VT/VF)
- **Low SvO₂/ScvO₂** despite adequate resuscitation
- **Abnormal echocardiogram** (reduced EF, ventricular dilation)

***

## Epidemiology

- **Incidence**: 20–60% in severe sepsis/septic shock; 18–40% specifically in septic shock cohorts [^1_5][^1_6]
- **Mortality**: Presence of SICM increases mortality to **70–90%** vs. ~30–40% without cardiac dysfunction [^1_6][^1_7]
- **Timing**: Typically develops **within 24–72 hours** of sepsis onset; recovery in **7–10 days** if patient survives [^1_2][^1_9]

***

## Pathophysiology

SICM is **multifactorial**, involving complex interactions between inflammatory mediators, mitochondrial dysfunction, autonomic dysregulation, and direct myocardial depression. [^1_5][^1_10][^1_6]

### Key Mechanisms

| Mechanism | Pathophysiological Effect |
| :-- | :-- |
| **Cytokine-mediated depression** | TNF-α, IL-1β, IL-6 → ↓ contractility via NO/cGMP pathway, ↓ β-adrenergic responsiveness [^1_5][^1_10] |
| **Mitochondrial dysfunction** | Impaired oxidative phosphorylation, ↓ ATP production, ↑ ROS → myocyte energy failure [^1_10][^1_9] |
| **Catecholamine toxicity** | Excessive sympathetic activation → cardiomyocyte apoptosis, β-receptor downregulation [^1_5] |
| **Nitric oxide (NO) excess** | ↑ cGMP → ↓ calcium sensitivity, ↓ myofilament responsiveness [^1_5][^1_6] |
| **Histone release** | Circulating histones → direct cardiotoxicity, endothelial dysfunction [^1_5] |
| **Coronary microvascular dysfunction** | Microthrombi, endothelial swelling → global ischemia despite normal coronaries [^1_10] |
| **Calcium handling abnormalities** | ↓ Sarcoplasmic reticulum Ca²⁺ uptake, ↓ troponin I phosphorylation → ↓ contractility [^1_5] |

### Hemodynamic Profile

- **LV dilation** with **increased compliance** (unlike cardiogenic shock)
- **Normal or low filling pressures** (CVP, PCWP)
- **Reduced EF** but **preserved or increased stroke volume** (due to dilation)
- **Biventricular involvement** common (LV + RV dysfunction) [^1_2][^1_10]

***

## Diagnosis

### 1. **Echocardiography** (Gold Standard) [^1_5][^1_2][^1_9][^1_7]

- **TTE** (transthoracic) or **TEE** (transesophageal) for better visualization
- **Key findings**:
    - LVEF <40–50%
    - LV dilation (↑ EDV, ↑ ESV)
    - RV dilation/dysfunction
    - Diastolic dysfunction (E/e′ ratio, impaired relaxation)
    - **Normal or low filling pressures** (distinguishes from cardiogenic shock)
- **Serial echo** to document reversibility (recovery in 7–10 days)


### 2. **Biomarkers**

- **Troponins** (I or T): Elevated in 50–70% of septic patients; correlates with mortality [^1_2][^1_9]
- **BNP/NT-proBNP**: Elevated; reflects ventricular stretch/dysfunction [^1_9]
- **Limitations**: Non-specific; must exclude ACS


### 3. **Hemodynamic Monitoring**

- **PAC** (pulmonary artery catheter): Low PCWP, low SVR, high/normal CO (early); ↓ CO in late/severe SICM
- **PiCCO/EV1000**: GEF (global ejection fraction), ITBV, stroke volume variation
- **ScvO₂/SvO₂**: Low despite resuscitation suggests cardiac dysfunction


### 4. **ECG**

- Non-specific ST-T changes, arrhythmias (AF, VT)
- **Exclude ACS** (STEMI/NSTEMI patterns)


### 5. **Coronary Angiography** (if ACS suspected)

- **Normal coronaries** support SICM diagnosis
- Reserve for high-risk patients with ECG changes, dynamic troponin rise

***

## Differential Diagnosis

| Condition | Distinguishing Features |
| :-- | :-- |
| **Acute Coronary Syndrome (ACS)** | ECG changes, coronary occlusion on angiography, regional wall motion abnormalities |
| **Cardiogenic Shock (primary)** | ↑ Filling pressures (PCWP >18 mmHg), history of HF, no sepsis trigger |
| **Stress Cardiomyopathy (Takotsubo)** | Apical ballooning, emotional/physical trigger, coronary spasm possible |
| **Myocarditis** | Viral prodrome, MRI findings (edema, LGE), endomyocardial biopsy |
| **PE with RV failure** | CT pulmonary angiography, RV strain on echo, hypoxemia disproportionate |
| **Pre-existing HF exacerbation** | History of HF, chronic LV dysfunction on prior echo |


***

## Management

**No specific targeted therapy exists**; management is **supportive** and focuses on **treating sepsis** while optimizing cardiac function. [^1_3][^1_4]

### 1. **Treat Underlying Sepsis** (Most Critical) [^1_5][^1_6]

- **Early antibiotics** (within 1 hour)
- **Source control** (drainage, debridement, device removal)
- **Guideline-directed sepsis care** (SSG 2021/2024)


### 2. **Hemodynamic Support**

#### Fluids

- **Cautious fluid resuscitation**; avoid overload (↑ mortality with positive fluid balance) [^1_5]
- Use **dynamic indices** (PLR, SVV, PPV) to assess fluid responsiveness
- **Target**: Euvolemia (not aggressive filling)


#### Vasopressors

- **Norepinephrine**: First-line for vasodilatory shock
- **Vasopressin**: Add-on to reduce NE dose
- **Phenylephrine**: Alternative (pure α-agonist; may ↓ CO)


#### Inotropes (if evidence of cardiac dysfunction + shock) [^1_5][^1_6]

| Agent | Mechanism | Pros | Cons |
| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |
| **Dobutamine** | β1/β2 agonist | ↑ CO, ↓ SVR | ↑ HR, ↑ O₂ demand, arrhythmias |
| **Epinephrine** | α/β agonist | ↑ MAP, ↑ CO | ↑ Lactate, arrhythmias, ↑ O₂ demand |
| **Levosimendan** | Ca²⁺ sensitizer + K⁺ channel opener | ↑ contractility without ↑ O₂ demand, vasodilation | Hypotension, limited availability [^1_6] |
| **Milrinone** | PDE3 inhibitor | Inotrope + vasodilator | Hypotension, renal clearance |

**Practical Approach**:

- **Dobutamine** (2–20 mcg/kg/min) if **low CO + adequate MAP**
- **Epinephrine** if **hypotension + low CO** (replaces NE + dobutamine)
- **Levosimendan** (if available): Consider in refractory cases; may have mortality benefit (limited data) [^1_6]


### 3. **Mechanical Circulatory Support** (Refractory Cases)

- **VA-ECMO**: For severe biventricular failure + refractory shock [^1_6]
- **IABP**: Limited role (afterload reduction in LV failure)
- **Impella**: Theoretical benefit; limited data in sepsis


### 4. **Avoid Harm**

- **Avoid excessive fluids** (↑ mortality with CVP >8–12 mmHg) [^1_5]
- **Avoid high-dose β-agonists** long-term (tachycardia, arrhythmias, ↑ O₂ demand)
- **Monitor troponins** (trend, not absolute value)
- **Serial echocardiography** to document recovery


### 5. **Adjunctive Therapies** (Investigational/Limited Evidence)

- **Corticosteroids** (hydrocortisone 200 mg/day): May improve shock reversal; unclear effect on SICM [^1_3]
- **Antioxidants** (vitamin C, thiamine): Theoretical benefit; no proven mortality benefit
- **Beta-blockers** (esmolol): HR control in tachycardic patients; may improve efficiency (controversial)

***

## Prognosis

- **Reversibility**: Cardiac function typically recovers within **7–10 days** in survivors [^1_5][^1_2][^1_9]
- **Mortality**: Presence of SICM increases mortality to **70–90%** vs. 30–40% without cardiac dysfunction [^1_6][^1_7]
- **Predictors of Poor Outcome**:
    - Persistent LV dysfunction >7 days
    - Severe RV involvement
    - Elevated troponins + lactate
    - Need for multiple vasopressors/inotropes
    - Older age, comorbidities

***

## Landmark Studies \& Evidence

| Study | Key Finding |
| :-- | :-- |
| **Parker et al. (1984)** [^1_5] | First modern description; radionuclide angiography showed ↓ LVEF, ↑ EDV in sepsis |
| **Boyd et al. (2011)** [^1_5] | Positive fluid balance + ↑ CVP associated with ↑ mortality in sepsis |
| **Vieillard-Baron et al. (2022)** [^1_3] | Review; emphasized lack of consensus definition, supportive care only |
| **Multiple echo studies (2015–2024)** [^1_2][^1_9][^1_8] | Confirmed LV dilation, ↓ EF, reversibility in 7–10 days; RV dysfunction common |


***

## Topic Pearls

- **SICM = reversible, non-ischemic ventricular dysfunction in sepsis**; LVEF <40–50%, LV dilation, normal/low filling pressures. [^1_5][^1_2][^1_8]
- **Diagnosis**: Echocardiography is gold standard; exclude ACS, pre-existing HF. [^1_5][^1_9][^1_7]
- **Pathophysiology**: Cytokines (TNF-α, IL-1β), NO, mitochondrial dysfunction, catecholamine toxicity. [^1_5][^1_10][^1_6]
- **Management**: Treat sepsis, cautious fluids, vasopressors (NE), inotropes (dobutamine/epi/levosimendan) if low CO. [^1_5][^1_6][^1_3]
- **Prognosis**: Mortality 70–90% with SICM; recovery in 7–10 days if patient survives. [^1_2][^1_6][^1_7]
- **No specific therapy**; supportive care + source control are key. [^1_3][^1_4]
- **Serial echo** to document reversibility; avoid overdiagnosis in patients with chronic HF. [^1_9][^1_7]

